@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@command{polygram} is a small, standard-library-only packaging and
orchestration layer that curates Claude agent skills driven by @emph{tags in
MEMORY}.  Notes captured the Mentat way accumulate tags; polygram reads those
tags, evaluates each candidate at a @dfn{gate}, synthesizes skill skeletons
only for the ones that pass, and mints installable bundles --- leaving a
growing on-disk history of @dfn{advice} behind every decision.

polygram does not author skill content for quality; it governs which themes
become skills and packages the result.  Mentat is the note discipline that
feeds it, and skilltank is the eye that spots skill-shaped patterns; polygram
is the forge between them.

@node Concepts
@chapter Concepts

@table @dfn
@item note
One fact in a Markdown file under @file{memory/}, optionally carrying tags.
@item tag
A label attached to a note, either an Org-style heading tag
(@samp{# Title :SKILL:}) or an inline hashtag (@samp{#shell-tooling}).
@item control tag
@samp{:SKILL:}, @samp{#skill-candidate}, @samp{#synth}.  These @emph{promote a
note's other tags} rather than becoming skills themselves.
@item candidate
A tag cluster, surfaced when it appears in enough notes (the
@option{--threshold}, default 2) or is force-surfaced by a control tag.
@item gate
The deterministic scorer that turns a candidate into a verdict.
@item verdict
One of @code{PASS}, @code{HOLD}, @code{REJECT}, with its reasoning.
@item advice
The dated Markdown record of a verdict, written under @file{advice/}.
@end table

@node Workspace
@chapter Workspace

A workspace is any directory containing a @file{.polygram} marker.

@example
.polygram        workspace marker
model.md         operating model (read first to resume context)
MEMORY.md        one-line-per-note index
memory/          one fact per file; tags drive synthesis
skills/          synthesized / curated skills
advice/          history of gate / dist / operator verdicts
dist/            minted archives + .skill bundles (gitignored)
externs/         cloned upstream sources
@end example

@node The pipeline
@chapter The pipeline

The core loop is
@example
notes -> candidates -> gate (PASS/HOLD/REJECT) -> advice -> synthesis -> dist
@end example

@section The gate
Each candidate is scored on cheap, explainable signals:

@multitable @columnfractions .7 .3
@headitem Signal @tab Effect
@item corroborated by two or more notes @tab +2
@item explicitly flagged by the operator @tab surfaces only (no score)
@item well-formed label (an all-letter word, or a hyphenated label of 4+ characters) @tab +1
@item overly generic label (@samp{misc}, @dots{}) @tab -2
@end multitable

@code{PASS} (score >= 3) synthesizes a skill skeleton; @code{HOLD} (>= 1)
records advice and waits for review; @code{REJECT} is logged and dropped.

@section Review
A @code{HOLD} verdict parks a candidate that lacks the signal to auto-pass.
Work the queue with @command{polygram review}: @option{--promote} overrides the
gate and synthesizes, @option{--reject} records a rejection, and
@option{--freeze-extern} pins the upstream a held candidate came from.

@node Distribution
@chapter Distribution

@command{polygram dist} packages curated skills.  It gathers the workspace
@file{skills/} plus any installed sources, writes a @file{MANIFEST} with a
SHA-256 per file, and mints archives.

@table @option
@item --format tar.gz|zip|skill|both|all
@code{both} (default) mints @file{.tar.gz} and @file{.zip}; @code{skill} mints a
folder-rooted, installable @file{<name>.skill} per skill; @code{all} mints
everything.
@item --skills-source DIR
An installed-skills root to include (repeatable).  When omitted, polygram reads
@env{POLYGRAM_SKILLS_SOURCE} (an @code{os.pathsep}-separated list).
@item --all-installed
Include all installed skills, not just the Mentat-family allowlist.
@item --name NAME / --dist-version VERSION / --out DIR
Archive base name, version stamp, and output directory.
@end table

On a name collision the workspace copy wins.  Editor and build cruft
(@file{__pycache__}, @file{*.pyc}, @file{*~}, @file{*.tmp}, Emacs
@file{#autosave#}, @file{.git}, @file{.DS_Store}) is excluded from every
archive and bundle.

@node Externs
@chapter Externs

An @dfn{extern} is an upstream skill source cloned under @file{externs/<name>/}
and recorded in @file{externs.json}.  An extern feeds candidates @emph{only
through its own @file{memory/} notes}, never arbitrary documents in the cloned
repository --- this prevents upstream noise from surfacing as skills.

An extern has a @dfn{kind}: @code{git} (a cloned remote) or @code{local} (a
hand-authored snapshot with no remote).  @dfn{Freezing} pins an extern at a
revision so @command{polygram extern sync} skips it, and a held candidate from a
frozen extern is flagged during review.

@node Command reference
@chapter Command reference

@table @command
@item polygram init
Create a workspace in the current directory.
@item polygram doctor
Report the runtime and validate the externs manifest.
@item polygram scan
Parse memory and list skill candidates.
@item polygram gate
Evaluate candidates and write advice (no synthesis).
@item polygram synth
Gate, then synthesize a skeleton for every PASS.
@item polygram review
List HOLD candidates; @option{--promote}, @option{--reject},
@option{--freeze-extern}.
@item polygram extern
Manage upstream sources: @code{add}, @code{list}, @code{sync}, @code{freeze},
@code{unfreeze}.
@item polygram list
List synthesized skills and recent advice.
@item polygram dist
Mint distributable archives and @file{.skill} bundles (@pxref{Distribution}).
@end table

Global options: @option{-C DIR}/@option{--dir DIR} operate as if started in
@var{DIR}; @option{--version} prints the version.

@node Quality control
@chapter Quality control

Synthesized skeletons are drafts, not shippable skills.  polygram's practice is
to pass them through a @dfn{sub-agent quality-control gate}: independent
reviewers --- a skill-builder reviewer (skill-creator and skilltank
conventions) and an SRE reviewer (provenance and safe-tooling) --- judge each
skeleton, and a skill ships only when both agree it is skill-shaped and sound.

This gate is what distinguishes a real skill from noise.  In the
2026-06-27 cycle it shipped @code{color-theory} (a WCAG contrast tool),
rejected @code{concise} (a preference, not a procedure), and rejected
@code{ffcc00} (a colour token scraped from an upstream's diagrams) --- then the
extern was frozen and a hex-token guard added so the noise could not recur.

